  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "TCS Balance Sheet and Cash Flow Statement Analysis",
  "scenario": "You are analyzing TCS's FY2024 consolidated financial statements (year ended 31-Mar-2025). Your Mumbai desk manager has asked you to verify the balance sheet equation, calculate key working capital metrics, and reconcile operating cash flow back to net profit to spot any cash-collection risk.",
  "instructions": "Complete the balance sheet verification and cash flow analysis by filling in the Answers sheet formulas. Calculate total assets, verify the A=L+E equation, compute working capital ratios, and trace the indirect cash flow method from PAT through operating cash flow.",
  "learning_objective": "Verify the balance sheet equation (Assets = Liabilities + Equity), calculate working capital metrics (Days Sales Outstanding, inventory turnover), and trace how net profit converts to operating cash flow using the indirect method to identify cash realization risk.",
  "hints": [
    "The balance sheet equation is simple: total all assets, total all liabilities and equity, and confirm they match exactly — it always balances by definition because every rupee of stuff was borrowed or owned.",
    "Working capital metrics divide balance sheet lines by P&L lines (e.g., receivables ÷ revenue per day to get DSO). Use SUMIF or direct cell references; remember to convert annual revenue to daily rate by dividing by 365.",
    "Operating cash flow reconciliation: start with PAT, add back depreciation (it reduced profit but didn't use cash), then adjust for working capital changes — if inventory grew, that spent cash (subtract); if payables grew, that saved cash (add)."
  ],
